    "part": "PART I — THE UNION AND ITS TERRITORY",
    "title": "Article 1: Name and Territory of the Union",
    "definition": "Article 1 of the Indian Constitution declares that India, that is Bharat, shall be a Union of States. The territory of India shall consist of the territories of the States, the Union Territories specified in the First Schedule, and such other territories as may be acquired.",
    "explanation": "Article 1 is the foundational provision of the Indian Constitution, establishing the very identity and territorial composition of the nation. The framers of the Constitution, under the leadership of Dr. B.R. Ambedkar, deliberately chose the phrase \"Union of States\" rather than \"Federation of States\" to emphasize the indestructible nature of the Indian Union. This distinction is of paramount constitutional significance.\n\nThe use of the term \"Union\" implies that the Indian federation is not the result of an agreement among sovereign states. Unlike the United States of America, where independent states came together to form a federation through a compact, the Indian Union was created by the Constitution itself. The states have no right to secede from the Union, and this principle has been upheld consistently by the Supreme Court of India.\n\nArticle 1 contains three clauses. Clause (1) provides the dual nomenclature — \"India, that is Bharat.\" This bilingual naming reflects the cultural duality of the nation, acknowledging both the English and Sanskrit traditions. The Constituent Assembly debated this extensively, with members like Seth Govind Das advocating exclusively for \"Bharat,\" while others preferred \"India\" for international recognition.\n\nClause (2) states that the territory of India shall comprise the territories of the States, the Union Territories specified in the First Schedule, and any other territory that may be acquired. This three-fold classification is crucial. The \"territories of the States\" are the federal units with their own legislatures and governments. \"Union Territories\" are directly administered by the Central Government, though some like Delhi and Puducherry have been granted legislative assemblies. The third category — \"acquired territories\" — provides constitutional flexibility for the Union to incorporate new territories through means recognized under international law, such as cession, conquest, or merger.\n\nClause (3) clarifies that the territory of any State shall be as specified in the First Schedule. This links Article 1 directly to the First Schedule, which lists all States and Union Territories along with their territorial extents.\n\nThe historical context of Article 1 is deeply intertwined with the integration of princely states. At the time of independence in 1947, India consisted of British India provinces and over 560 princely states. The monumental task of integrating these princely states into the Indian Union was accomplished largely through the efforts of Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel and V.P. Menon. Article 1, read with Article 370 (now abrogated) and Article 371 series, provided the constitutional framework for this integration.\n\nThe phrase \"Union of States\" also carries implications for the distribution of legislative, executive, and judicial powers. Since India is a Union, residuary powers vest in the Centre under Article 248, unlike in a true federation where residuary powers might vest in the constituent units. This centralized structure was a deliberate choice, influenced by the Partition, communal tensions, and the need for a strong central authority to maintain national integrity.\n\nFurthermore, Article 1 must be read in conjunction with Articles 2, 3, and 4. Article 2 empowers Parliament to admit new States or establish new States on terms it deems fit. Article 3 grants Parliament the power to form new States, alter boundaries, and change names of existing States. Article 4 provides that laws made under Articles 2 and 3 shall not be considered amendments under Article 368. Together, these provisions establish a dynamic territorial framework.\n\nThe scope of Article 1 extends to territorial waters, continental shelf, and exclusive economic zones as defined under the Territorial Waters, Continental Shelf, Exclusive Economic Zone and Other Maritime Zones Act, 1976. The Supreme Court has clarified that sovereignty over territorial waters is part of India's territory under Article 1.",
